
# Task 02 – Return by Reference and Its Dangers

## Aim
To understand returning references from functions and the dangers of dangling references.

## 1. Return by Reference
The highestMark() function returns a reference to the highest element in the marks array.

This allows the caller to modify the original array element directly.

Example:
highestMark(marks, 5) += 2;

The highest mark changes from 91 to 93.

## 2. Dangling Reference
A dangling reference occurs when a function returns a reference to a local variable.

Example:
int& dangerousFunction()
{
    int value = 50;
    return value;
}

The local variable is destroyed when the function ends. Therefore, the returned reference is invalid.

Using it causes undefined behaviour.

Compiler command:
g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -Wreturn-local-addr main.cpp -o task02

The compiler may issue a warning about returning a reference to a local variable.

## 3. Reference, Const Reference and Copy

Reference:
Returns the original variable and allows modification.

Const Reference:
Returns the original variable but prevents modification through that reference.

Copy:
Returns a separate value. Changes to the copy do not affect the original array.

## 4. Vector Reallocation
A vector can increase its capacity when new elements are inserted.

During reallocation, existing elements may move to a new memory location. References obtained before reallocation can become invalid.

Therefore, references should not be used after an operation that may reallocate the vector.

## 5. Alternative Concept

Using an index:

int highestIndex(const int marks[], int size)
{
    int highest = 0;

    for (int i = 1; i < size; i++)
    {
        if (marks[i] > marks[highest])
            highest = i;
    }

    return highest;
}

Calling code:
int index = highestIndex(marks, 5);
marks[index] += 2;

Using a pointer:
int* highestPointer = &marks[index];
*highestPointer += 2;

## 6. Complexity Analysis

Finding the highest mark:

Time Complexity: O(n)
Space Complexity: O(1)

Returning by reference:
O(1) additional parameter/return overhead.

Returning by copy:
O(1) for an integer.

Returning an index:
O(1) return overhead.

Returning a pointer:
O(1) return overhead.

The search itself takes O(n) in every approach.

## Result
The program demonstrates returning a reference to an array element, modifying the highest mark, and understanding the risks of dangling references and vector reallocation.
